, "rationale": "The district's five-point Republican lean and a mostly Lean-R expert consensus favor Elijah, and markets price him at roughly two-to-one. Dalton's four-to-one fundraising advantage, outside support, and a Democratic-friendly midterm environment keep the race close enough to stay in tossup territory.", "watch": "Fresh general-election polling or a surge of outside spending on either side, or signs that Elijah's post-primary consolidation is faltering, could quickly move this open-seat race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Cory Mills (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Florida's 7th District is an open seat in 2026 after Republican Cory Mills lost his party's nomination to Ryan Elijah, a first-time nominee. The district leans Republican by about five points, and most expert raters call the race Lean or Likely Republican, though The Economist rates it a Democratic flip and Silver Bulletin calls it a tossup. Prediction markets give Elijah roughly a two-to-one edge.\n\nDemocrat Bale Dalton has out-raised Elijah by about four to one, holds a cash advantage, and benefits from roughly $700,000 in outside support with no outside spending against him so far. Public polling is nearly nonexistent; a May Democratic-sponsored survey showed Dalton at 41 percent in a hypothetical matchup. The campaign has centered on affordability and attacks over Dalton's positions, while Elijah works to consolidate support after a divisive primary. A favorable national environment for Democrats keeps the seat in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4211, "margin": -1.21}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4651, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.3497, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.3248, "margin": -3.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2949, "margin": -3.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.3493, "margin": -2.0}]}